/*! \page page_faq Frequently Asked Questions

    \tableofcontents

    \section faq_errors runtime errors

    When PB3D or POST stops on an error, it prints the error message, the trace of the calling routines and the line <tt>ERROR CODE [code]</tt>.

    <table>
    <caption id="faq_errors_tab">Table 1. frequently encountered errors</caption>
    <tr>
        <th> error </th><th> solution </th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>"Cannot read user-provided file" </td> <td> The input file contains a variable that is not part of the Fortran namelist <tt>inputdata_PB3D</tt> or <tt>inputdata_POST</tt>, or a value of the wrong type. The message that follows comes from the Fortran run-time library and usually names the variable. Check the spelling against \ref page_inputs. An input that PB3D does not read, such as <tt>norm_disc_style_sol</tt>, also gives this error. </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>"BC_style(1) has to be 1: the perturbation is set to zero at the inner end" or "BC_style(2) has to be 1 (fixed boundary) or 2 (free boundary with vacuum)" </td> <td> \c BC_style accepts only these values: <tt>BC_style(1) = 1</tt> deep in the plasma, and <tt>BC_style(2) = 1</tt> (fixed boundary) or <tt>BC_style(2) = 2</tt> (free boundary, the default) at the plasma edge (see \ref page_inputs). </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>"Free-boundary 3-D runs need alpha_style 2 (multiple field lines): the vacuum BEM requires n_alpha > 1" </td> <td> For a VMEC equilibrium with a free boundary, the vacuum response is calculated with a 3-D boundary-element method on the field lines, which needs several field lines. Use <tt>alpha_style = 2</tt> (the default for VMEC) with \c n_alpha larger than 1. The related messages "3-D vacuum needs at least 2 field lines (n_alpha > 1)" and "3-D vacuum needs at least 4 points per field line" ask for a larger \c n_alpha and a larger \c min_n_par_X, respectively. </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>"The memory limit is too low, need more than N MB" </td> <td> The equilibrium jobs (see \ref overview_jobs) cannot be made small enough to fit in the memory limit \c max_tot_mem. Increase \c max_tot_mem. For HELENA, there is always a single equilibrium job, so the whole calculation has to fit in this limit. </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>"The output is from PB3D version [version]: need at least version [version]" </td> <td> POST, and PB3D when it continues from an earlier run (<tt>rich_restart_lvl</tt> larger than 1), only read HDF5 output written by a PB3D version at least as recent as \c min_PB3D_version in num_vars. Run PB3D again with the current version. </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>"Asking to integrate with N points. Need at least 2" </td> <td> The routine calc_int() integrates with the trapezoidal rule, which needs at least 2 points. The message means that an integral was requested over a single point. The trace of the error message shows which routine asked for the integral. It might be that the number of points in a grid is lower than you expect because the grid is divided over the MPI processes or the equilibrium jobs; in this case, increase the number of points, use fewer processes, or allow more memory (see \c max_tot_mem in \ref page_inputs). </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>"Aument number of modes or choose finer equilibrium" </td> <td> This message follows the line "Mode N is not present in any flux surface" from setup_modes(). The range of secondary mode numbers at every flux surface is either user-specified, for \c X_style 1, or chosen automatically around the most resonant values for \c X_style 2 (see \ref page_inputs). This implies that, on the other hand, every secondary mode should have a normal range in which it is present. The code checks whether this is indeed the case. It could, for example, be that a certain mode number is not present anywhere, when the normal grid in which it is tabulated is too coarse. A solution is therefore to choose a finer grid. Alternatively, for \c X_style 1, the range can be adapted, and for \c X_style 2 the number of modes \c n_mod_X. Note that the particular grid that this discussion is about can be found out by the trace of the error message. If it results from the solution driver, it concerns the solution grid, and if it results from the perturbation driver, it concerns the perturbation grid. The solution grid is specified by its range through \c min_r_sol and \c max_r_sol and the number of points \c n_r_sol, and the perturbation grid by the \c X_grid_style. See \ref page_inputs. </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>"The code has not been tested satisfactorily for decreasing normal coordinate. See plot r_F" </td> <td> PB3D stops (error code 3) if the normal flux coordinate at the last flux surface is not larger than at the first (see calc_eq()). It plots this coordinate in <tt>r_F</tt>. For VMEC with the poloidal flux as normal coordinate (the default), this coordinate is the integral of the rotational transform \f$\iota\f$ times the derivative of the toroidal flux, so it decreases when \f$\iota\f$ and the toroidal flux have opposite signs; this is the case, for example, for a VMEC file with a negative toroidal flux and a positive \f$\iota\f$. A workaround is to run the equilibrium solver again with inverted fields and currents. </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>"The code has to be adapted for reversed-shear regions still. See plot Dq_saf" </td> <td> PB3D stops (error code 3) if the derivative of the safety factor changes sign in the plasma (see calc_eq()). It plots this derivative in <tt>Dq_saf</tt>. The routines that calculate the resonant flux surfaces, such as calc_res_surf(), have to be extended for reversed-shear regions; this matters mostly for \c X_style 2 (fast) and \c X_grid_style 3 (enriched). </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>"EPS couldn't find a solution. Maybe you should increase the number of parallel points." with error code 92, after PETSc printed "Could not locate a solver type for factorization type LU and matrix type mpibaij" </td> <td> This happens with more than one MPI process. The eigenvalue solver uses shift-and-invert, which needs a direct LU factorization of a distributed matrix, and the built-in LU of PETSc only works on one process. Build PETSc with MUMPS (for example with the configure options <tt>--download-mumps --download-parmetis --download-metis</tt>); PETSc then uses MUMPS by itself. Alternatively, run on a single process. </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>"Zero pivot in LU factorization" </td> <td> This message comes from PETSc. There exist cases in which the eigenvalue solver fails when <tt>n_mod_X = 1</tt>, for unknown reasons. Increase <tt>n_mod_X</tt> or play with other parameters, such as \c norm_disc_prec_sol. </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>\c jump_to_sol does not work</td> <td> Jumping over the equilibrium and perturbation drivers straight to the solution only works if the magnetic integrals of the Richardson level \c rich_restart_lvl are present in the HDF5 output, and if only solution parameters, such as the boundary conditions, are changed. With <tt>X_grid_style = 2</tt>, the solution grid is the perturbation grid, so the number of points in the solution, \c n_r_sol, cannot be changed, as in this case the equilibrium and perturbation drivers need to be recalculated. With \c X_grid_style 1 or 3, the solution grid is set up again from \c n_r_sol, \c min_r_sol and \c max_r_sol, and the magnetic integrals are interpolated in it (see \ref detail_PB3D). New input values only take effect when jumping at <tt>rich_restart_lvl = 1</tt>, because PB3D then writes the input variables to the HDF5 output again; at a higher level, PB3D takes them from the HDF5 output of the earlier run (see the last entry in \ref faq_problems). </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>The solution suffers from numerical oscillations</td> <td> Change the limits of the solution grid \c min_r_sol (and possibly \c max_r_sol). The radial derivative in the solution is a left finite difference over the point itself and the \c norm_disc_prec_sol points to its left (see set_bc()). </td>
    </tr>
    </table>

    \section faq_problems common problems

    <table>
    <caption id="faq_problems_tab">Table 2. common problems</caption>
    <tr>
        <th> problem </th><th> solution </th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>code compilation stuck</td> <td> Especially with the Intel compiler, it has been found that sometimes the compiler seems to be stuck, especially for \c eq_ops. A solution is to use less optimization through \c -O2, for example with the CMake build type <tt>RelWithDebInfo</tt>, which uses \c -O2 for all files. </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><tt>cmake --build</tt> does not start compiling</td> <td> With CMake 3.31 and the Fortran headers of PETSc 3.25, the dependency scan of CMake loops on <tt>petscmath.h</tt> and <tt>petscsys.h</tt>, which include each other, for a long time and with a large amount of memory. CMake 4.3 and 4.4 scan the source tree without this problem. </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>A Debug build with gfortran 14 has no bounds checking</td> <td> gfortran 14.2 and 14.3 miscompile the bounds check of some array subscripts in PB3D. For gfortran 14, CMake therefore builds the Debug configuration with <tt>-fcheck=all,no-bounds</tt> instead of <tt>-fcheck=all</tt>, and prints a message about it. Use another gfortran version to build with bounds checking. </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>Random crashes on macOS, with SIGTRAP or a failed <tt>H5Fcreate</tt></td> <td> These come from a heap overrun in PSPLINE (<tt>r8genxpkg</tt> writes past its array for three unevenly spaced points), which the macOS memory allocator traps at a later allocation. Build PSPLINE with <tt>Libraries/build_pspline.sh</tt> of the PB3D repository, which applies the patch <tt>Libraries/pspline-r8genxpkg-nx3.patch</tt>. </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><tt>mpirun</tt> crashes on macOS</td> <td> With the OpenMPI 5 of Homebrew, <tt>mpirun</tt> itself can crash while it detects the hardware topology. A single-process run works by starting the executable directly, without <tt>mpirun</tt>, for example <tt>./PB3D PB3D.input wout.nc</tt>. </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>unphysical eigenvector</td> <td> For coarse equilibrium grids and/or strongly varying safety factors, and when using the fast version (i.e. with \c n_mod_X), it is possible that the resonating mode number range varies too quickly, leading to inaccurate interpolation of tensorial perturbation quantities in the solution grid. Try using \c X_grid_style 3, where enrichment of the equilibrium grid is used to set up the perturbation grid. If this does not help, additionally lower \c max_njq_change from its default value. </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>checking convergence</td> <td> Richardson extrapolation (\c max_it_rich larger than 1) checks the convergence in the number of parallel points. For \c X_style 2, the window of mode numbers follows the resonant mode numbers across the plasma, and the coupling between a mode number that leaves the window and one that enters it is not kept; double \c n_mod_X once to check the result. For a free boundary with VMEC, the 3-D vacuum also has to converge in the number of field lines: the changelog in the code's <tt>README.md</tt> records, as a rule, at least 128 field lines and about 36 parallel points per poloidal wavelength. A fixed-boundary run (<tt>BC_style(2) = 1</tt>) does not calculate the vacuum. </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>low accuracy for energy reconstruction</td> <td> Energy reconstruction tries to see whether by calculating the total volume integral of the perturbed potential energy and dividing it by the total volume integral of the kinetic energy of the perturbation, the eigenvalue can be recovered.
        This gives a lot of insight in the processes that drive the instabilities, by looking at the individual terms of these integrals, and is a good check on the internal consistency of the PB3D code.
        For a free boundary, the potential energy includes the vacuum term (see \ref output_file_E_rec in \ref page_outputs).
        Note that for axisymmetric equilibria, the volume integral reduces to a surface integral in a poloidal cross-section.
        It is actually very hard to perform a good energy reconstruction because the techniques used differ from the ones in the PB3D code, where the normal derivatives of the eigenvector are performed using finite differences, while in the energy reconstruction direct volume integrals are used.
        To get the best possible results, therefore, it is important to achieve full Richardson convergence in the number of parallel points (by increasing \c max_it_rich and/or \c min_n_par_X), as well as convergence in the number of secondary mode harmonics (by increasing \c n_mod_X).
        Doing this, for axisymmetric cases, the code should return quite accurate results.
        These can furthermore be improved by choosing a finer post-processing grid (see \c n_theta_plot and \c n_zeta_plot).
        It is, however, possible that a small fraction, possibly of the order of a percent remains in the relative difference.
        This is probably due to the fact that in PB3D for \c X_style 2 (fast), the stencil is cut when the normal index falls out of its mode range (see insert_block_mat()). </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>Changing input does not work for Richardson extrapolation</td> <td> You cannot change the inputs as Richardson extrapolation depends on having these constant (with the exception of the number of points in the parallel integrals, but this is done internally in PB3D). When PB3D continues from an earlier run (<tt>rich_restart_lvl</tt> larger than 1), it reads the input variables that the run with <tt>rich_restart_lvl = 1</tt> wrote to the <tt>in</tt> group of the HDF5 output (see init_rich() and reconstruct_pb3d_in()). These include the solution grid (\c n_r_sol, \c min_r_sol, \c max_r_sol), \c BC_style, \c norm_disc_prec_sol, the mode numbers and the field lines, so different values for these in the input file have no effect. Inputs that are not stored there, such as \c max_it_rich, \c tol_rich and \c tol_SLEPC, can be changed. </td>
    </tr>
    </table>
*/
